    {
      "id": "GPkg",
      "code_hash": "b3e2b4d6ce5581407d704a4a3b1c2e4b",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">get_all_towns_in_county: This function takes one input, a county name like 'Middlesex'. It returns a list of dictionaries, one per town in that county, covering every page of results from the API \u2014 not just the first 100 towns. The line I had to ask about was the while True: loop with its break condition \u2014 I wasn't sure why it needed to keep requesting more pages instead of asking once, until I realized a single request only returns up to 100 towns at a time, so the loop keeps going until every page has been collected.\"</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">get_town_history: This function takes two inputs: a town's dor_code and a specific year. It returns a single dictionary with that one town's figures for exactly that year, not a list. The line I had to ask about was reply.json()[\"years\"][0] \u2014 I wasn't sure why there was a [0] there since I'm passing the same year for both from_year and to_year. It's because the API always returns a list of years, even when that list only has one item in it, so [0] pulls that single year's record out.\"</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">For the change and prediction: I'd suggest switching county from \"Middlesex\" to a different county, like \"Norfolk\" \u2014 predict something like: \"I expect a different set of towns to appear on the list, since Norfolk's home values and tax bills are different from Middlesex's, and the cheapest town will likely be a different name entirely.\"</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
